BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 708.0 Td (    # The Parable of the Sun and the River) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 698.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 688.0 Td (    In the first days, the Sun held all warmth in her golden hands, and the River held all water) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 678.0 Td (       in his deep channels. Each was rich alone, but the world between them withered.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 668.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 658.0 Td (    One morning, the Sun chose to give. She poured her warmth downward-not all of it, for she ne) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 648.0 Td (      eded some to sustain herself, but enough to show faith. As her rays touched the River, som) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 638.0 Td (      ething miraculous happened: the warmth multiplied threefold in the water, creating mists t) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 628.0 Td (      hat rose and danced.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 618.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 608.0 Td (    Now the River faced a choice. He held triple what the Sun had given, transformed into vapor ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 598.0 Td (      and motion and life-giving potential. He could keep it all, letting it flow only through h) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 588.0 Td (      is own channels. Or he could return a portion skyward.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 578.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 568.0 Td (    The River chose reciprocity. He sent generous clouds back to the Sun, who gained both her wa) Tj ET
